  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 AM EDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\n- Dry though Saturday\n\nDrier air is working in behind the cold front that has recently\nmoved through the state. The upper level low will continue to\nrotate a few shortwave troughs this afternoon and early Friday.\nOutlier solutions try to pop up some showers and thunderstorms \nthis afternoon and Friday afternoon, but overall probabilities are\nlow (less than 20 percent) for any one location. Pressure heights\nincrease Friday night into Saturday as high pressure builds to \nour south. Highs into Saturday will be in the 70s to low 80s.\n\n- Periods of Showers and Thunderstorms Saturday Night into Next\n  Week\n\nWe'll remain on the northern edge of high pressure into next week.\nThere aren't any significant shortwaves moving through, but the\nlow level jet will gradually shift into the area bringing us\nmultiple chances for showers and thunderstorms starting Saturday\nnight and continuing into next week. Our chances will ebb and \nflow with the low level jet as it will be our main source of lift \nalong with advecting in some high precipitable water values (1.5 \nto 2.0 inches) and dew points (60s). There are differences in \nwhere exactly the low level jet sets up which will impact who sees\nthe best chances for rain and thunderstorms. There is the \npotential for heavy rainfall, although probabilities for higher \namounts for any one location are low due to the uncertainty of \nwhere rain and thunderstorms are most likely to track.",
